---
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import editor from '../../assets/img/eaton-editor.png';
import compare from '../../assets/img/eaton-compare.png';
import confirm from '../../assets/img/eaton-confirm.png';
import monitor from '../../assets/img/eaton-monitor.png';
import monitorLight from '../../assets/img/eaton-monitor-light.png';
import monitorDark from '../../assets/img/eaton-monitor-dark.png';

// The short case. The deck is one sentence, the ending; the hero is the shipped editor cut to
// its first four rows. Every later frame is a region of one of the Webflow page's own images cut
// to the thing its paragraph names, and the weight follows the argument: the Compare view whole
// under decision 1; the confirmation in situ under decision 2, with
// the switch in the same toolbar; the two drafts and the shipped monitor cut to their charts and
// set three across under decision 3, so the change from four axes to one and the colours read at
// one glance. The canvas
// editor that lost the test isn't shown: only what launched can be. The hand-off is a sentence
// in decision 2, as Heygen's is in its decision 1.
---
<Case
  title="Eaton — Kyle Xu"
  description="UX Design Intern at Eaton, June–August 2022. RapidLinkX, an IoT configuration tool for airport conveyor systems: a parameter editor and a fault monitor for airport engineers. In a comparative usability test the drag-and-drop editor was 35% faster and made 15% more errors, so the form shipped. 14 component variants added to Eaton's design system; in usability testing, operational efficiency +49.7% and error rates −36.8%."
  ogImage="/og-eaton.jpg"
  eyebrow="Eaton · UX Design Intern · June – August 2022"
  deck="The faster of the two editors we tested, 35% quicker, made 15% more errors, so the slower one shipped."
  next={{ title: 'Heygen', to: '/projects/heygen' }}
>
  <Fragment slot="claim">A configuration tool for the drives that run an airport's conveyors.</Fragment>
  <Figure slot="hero" src={editor} name="The parameter editor" evidence="Shipped" loading="eager"
    alt="RapidLinkX's parameter editor: a blue header and toolbar with a Real-Time switch turned off, linked drives listed on the left with one selected, and a table of starred parameters with a value field, range, default and display checkbox on each row"
    caption="Real-Time off in the toolbar, Starred filtering the table, Compare at the right." />

  <section class="col sec snap" aria-label="In thirty seconds">
    <dl class="facts">
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Scope</dt>
        <dd>RapidLinkX, an IoT configuration tool for the drives in airport conveyor systems, used by airport engineers: a parameter editor and a fault monitor over USB. I was the primary designer on the <span class="nb">day-to-day</span> product design; my mentor planned the work and carried communication across teams.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Constraints</dt>
        <dd>The hardware was designed in parallel, so we reviewed with its team every week or two and redrew what had moved. Regional sales, dealers, <span class="nb">in-house</span> engineering and an external development team all had a say. Pandemic restrictions kept us from the engineers themselves; secondary research and stakeholder workshops stood in.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Outcome</dt>
        <dd>In usability testing of the shipped tool, operational efficiency up 49.7% and error rates down 36.8%.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-editor">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-editor"><span class="n">1</span>The slower editor</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The comparative usability test split its participants between a <span class="nb">drag-and-drop</span> canvas and this form. The canvas was quicker. It also produced more wrong values, and a wrong value on a baggage line stops the line, so the form went forward and some of the speed came back in two smaller places. Starred parameters filter the table down to the ones that matter on a check. Compare sets the live values beside a saved configuration or the defaults, so a drift shows as a difference rather than a number to hold in your head. Eaton's NDA covers what didn't launch, so the canvas isn't here.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure src={compare} name="Compare" evidence="Shipped"
        alt="The parameter comparison view: a breadcrumb reading Parameter Configuration, Parameter Comparison, the title Compare with default values, and a table with a current value beside the default and the range on each row"
        caption="Live values beside the defaults; a saved configuration can take the defaults' place." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-write">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-write"><span class="n">2</span>Writing to a drive</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>An engineer on a routine round wants the tool linked and watching. One at a drive that has just failed wants nothing written until they've decided what.</p>
      <p>So <span class="nb">real-time</span> writing is a switch, off when the tool opens, and both routes, the switch and Write In, pass through one confirmation, with a backup of the drive's configuration ticked before anything goes. A running write says so in the header and asks not to be unplugged. These states went to the external developers in the last six weeks as annotated boards with one protocol for questions; they were new to Eaton's Brightlayer system, and what it didn't have became 14 component variants added to it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure src={confirm} name="Confirm Write-in" evidence="Shipped"
        alt="The editor's toolbar with the Real-Time switch off and a Write In button at the right, and beneath it the parameter table with a dialog over its right half: Confirm Write-in, a note that changes will be written in real time to the connected inverter, a ticked checkbox to back up the config of the connected device, a config name reading DC 01, and Cancel and Confirm buttons"
        caption="Real-Time off, the dialog up from Write In, the backup ticked." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-monitor">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-monitor"><span class="n">3</span>The monitor, in colour</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The same test set a <span class="nb">text-heavy</span> monitor against a <span class="nb">colour-coded</span> chart, and with the chart participants found errors 40% faster. I drew it twice, light and dark, each with the four channels stacked on axes of their own. The dark one read better; the light one is what Brightlayer looks like, so it went forward. By the time it shipped the four traces shared one axis, where a channel can be read against the others, and their colours had been <span class="nb">re-tuned</span> to stay apart once the lines crossed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="three" caption="Four channels on white, on dark, and as shipped, on one axis. The drafts carry Chinese labels; the tool shipped in English."
        items={[
          { src: monitorLight, name: 'Light', evidence: 'Prototype', alt: 'The light draft of the monitor, cut to its channel cards and chart: four cards in blue, green, purple and orange with live values, four more unset, and beneath them four traces stacked on separate axes over a white grid' },
          { src: monitorDark, name: 'Dark', evidence: 'Concept', alt: 'The dark draft of the monitor, the same region: the four coloured channel cards, the four traces stacked over a dark grid, and a legend of the four channels floating over the chart' },
          { src: monitor, name: 'The monitor', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'The shipped monitor, cut to its channel cards and chart: channels 1 to 4 in blue, green, purple and orange with live values and min and max, channels 5 to 8 reading Please Select, and beneath them the four traces on one axis over a hundred seconds' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="close">
    <p class="stance">The slower editor was the right one to ship.</p>
    <p>A demo shows the canvas's speed. It doesn't show the wrong value that sits on a drive until a line stops, or the round it takes to find it and put it right, and that round costs more than the canvas saved.</p>
  </Fragment>
</Case>
